            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attack hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")



** The old bathroom exhaust fan on the 2nd floor



** The older wood door between the house &amp; the garage



** The ductwork for the furnace is compromised to the exterior of the heated interior space (garage, crawl ???)



** The current 5" chimney &amp; 5" combustion air supply pipe for the furnace



Savings: I modeled a simple 10% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 156 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.5 GJ. You may even achieve a 20% reduction, creating further energy savings.

</airSeal>
                <doors>

Your house has good quality windows &amp; doors throughout, except for that older wood door (with a single pane of glass for the window) that separates the house from the garage. Not only is this door have a very poor R-Value, it is also not well sealed &amp; it whistled loudly during my blower door air leakage test. I recommend you upgrade this older door to an ENERGY STAR?????? rated "exterior" insulated door for our climate zone.



Rebates:

By replacing this older door with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified unit rated for our climate zone, you could potentially  save $ 70 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.1 GJ.</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank that was installed 2.5 years ago. As the cost of electricity is expensive in Yellowknife &amp; electric hot water tanks usually have a lifespan of about 5-7 years here, I have modeled an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher. One of the most popular units deployed in Yellowknife recently has an energy factor of 0.97, so I modeled one of these for you.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.97, could potentially save $ 1,117 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a recently installed new oil furnace (83.3% efficiency) that is the primary heating system for both the main and 2nd floor, and you recently had the thermostat relocated to the main floor. Your house has a wood pellet stove on the upper floor that (because of the central open stairwell between the floors) may just add heat to an already overheated upper floor. The challenge is to move that heat from the upper floor back to the lower floor.



When using the furnace to heat, you want to have the primary cold air return to the furnace being at floor level on the main floor. When using the pellet stove upstairs, you want to have an optional high wall return near the ceiling of the upper floor &amp; then operate the furnace in "circulation fan" mode to redistribute the upstairs heat to the lower floors. Having the ability to alternate between the 2 return air locations (by having the ability to open or close each one) allows you the ability to achieve your desired heat distribution. Of course, you may also need to close some of the heat supply registers on the upper floor to force more warm air into the lower level.



As discussed, there appears to be many heat duct issues that need to be investigated further by a heating contractor:

- what is that ductwork for in the crawlspace, as it is nowhere near the location of the furnace

- the heat supply into the garage is not legal and needs to be closed off

- why was there so much air coming in through the ductwork during my blower door test. The heat supply in the garage was plugged, so there must be other locations where air is getting in from outside the building envelope of the interior heated living space

- The ductwork along the ceiling of the garage is not sealed or insulated. There does not appear to be an air/vapour/gas barrier on the ceiling of the garage. Perhaps the header space above the wall that adjoins the house &amp; garage is also not sealed? 



The current furnace vents up a 5" chimney and there is also a 5" combustion air supply pipe to the furnace room. In addition, there is a 6" fresh air supply interconnected to the return air of the furnace. Each of these locations has a damper electronically interconnected to the burner of the furnace. I could see the damper on the combustion air supply was in the closed position &amp; I assume the one inside the fresh air supply was also in the closed position (if not, this could be part of the air leakage I noticed coming in through the ductwork).



Even though you just installed a new furnace, it is near the very bottom of the energy efficiency scale. As discussed, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This type of furnace will eliminate the current 5" chimney and the 5" combustion air supply pipe, however the 6" fresh air supply pipe should be maintained.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1,021 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 24.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs and fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has various components that contribute to ventilation:

        - the kitchen range hood fan vents to the outside

        - there is a bathroom fans that vents to the outside

        - there is a clothes dryer that is not yet installed but will exhaust to the exterior

        - there is a 6" fresh air (make-up air) supply pipe connected to the return plenum of the furnace



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat. The dryer vent on the outside of the house is a model I have not yet seen, &amp; I'm suspicious it may be prone to becoming quickly blocked with wet/frozen dryer lint during the winter months ??



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood fan, bathroom fan, but not the dryer as it was not yet installed), indicated a pressure drop of 1.8 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace does atmospherically vent up a chimney, and could be susceptible to back-drafting combustion gases into your house. The wood pellet stove has a directly connected/dedicated outside combustion air pipe.



If you upgrade the furnace to sealed combustion unit (as outlined elsewhere in this report), that type of furnace is not susceptible to back-drafting .......... and the current chimney &amp; combustion air supply pipe for the existing oil furnace can be eliminated.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre delivered

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high trans</AdditionalComments>
